==English==
[[File:DangerousShelfCloud.jpg|right|thumb|A [[shelf cloud]] marking the leading edge of a '''derecho''']]

===Etymology===
{{lbor|en|es|derecho||straight}}. First used by {{w|Gustavus Detlef Hinrichs}} in 1888, apparently coined to distinguish the winds from tornadoes, which rotate;<ref>Ray Wolf, [http://www.crh.noaa.gov/dvn/?n=hinrichs "A Brief History of Gustavus Hinrichs, Discoverer of the DERECHO"]</ref><ref>Ammon Shea, [https://web.archive.org/web/20120728011846/http://blog.oxforddictionaries.com/2012/07/all-about-the-weather/ "All about the weather"], OxfordWords blog, 25 July 2012</ref> compare {{m|en|tornado}}. {{doublet|en|direct}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/dəˈɹeɪ.t͡ʃoʊ/|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-derecho.ogg|a=US}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|s|es}}

# {{lb|en|meteorology|US}} A [[windstorm]] characterized by [[strong]], [[straight]]-[[line]] [[wind]]s.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2015|author=Chris J. Peterson; Jeffery B. Cannon; Christopher M. Godfrey|chapter=5: First Steps Toward Defining the Wind Disturbance Regime in Central Hardwoods Forests|editors=Cathryn H. Greenberg; Beverly S. Collins|title=Natural Disturbances and Historic Range of Variation|publisher=Springer|pageurl=https://books.google.com.au/books?id=ySHUCgAAQBAJ&pg=PA116&dq=%22derechos%22+wind&hl=en&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwiE9LrWlMH7AhWl8HMBHUxQCvgQ6AF6BAhOEAI#v=onepage&q=%22derechos%22%20wind&f=false|page=116
|passage=Combining all of the above, the Arkansas Valley, Ouachita Mountains, Interior Plateau, and Southwest Appalachian ecoregions have the greatest risk of wind damage from tornadoes and '''derechos''', whereas weakening hurricanes are an important disturbance, especially in the Piedmont ecoregion.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2016|author=Bill Streever|title=And Soon I Heard a Roaring Wind|pageurl=https://books.google.com.au/books?id=E5wuCwAAQBAJ&pg=PT242&dq=%22derechos%22+wind&hl=en&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwiE9LrWlMH7AhWl8HMBHUxQCvgQ6AF6BAgHEAI#v=onepage&q=%22derechos%22%20wind&f=false|page=unnumbered|publisher=Hachette
|passage='''Derechos''' have winds as powerful as those of some hurricanes and tornadoes. "'''Derecho'''" comes from the Spanish word meaning "straight."}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2022|author=Alexander Gates|title=Earth's Fury|pageurl=https://books.google.com.au/books?id=Til4EAAAQBAJ&pg=PT487&dq=%22derechos%22+wind&hl=en&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwiE9LrWlMH7AhWl8HMBHUxQCvgQ6AF6BAhVEAI#v=onepage&q=%22derechos%22%20wind&f=false|page=unnumbered|publisher=Wiley
|passage=A '''derecho''' can be as destructive as a weak to moderate tornado, but the damage is mainly the result of straight-line rather than rotating winds.<br>However, tornadoes can also form along the bow of a '''derecho'''. '''Derechos''' can sometimes be recognized as a bank of ominous-looking "shelf" clouds that appear darker than normal clouds.}}

